Q2) Why is Java platform independent?

Java is platform independent because Java source code is compiled into bytecode instead of directly into machine-specific code.

This bytecode can run on any operating system that has a compatible JVM.

For example, the same Java bytecode can run on Windows, Linux, or macOS with their respective JVMs.

Simple example:

Same Java bytecode
       ↓
 -------------------------
 ↓          ↓           ↓
Windows    Linux       Mac
 JVM        JVM         JVM

 --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Q3) What is JVM?

JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.

It is responsible for executing Java bytecode.

The JVM converts bytecode into machine-level instructions that the operating system can understand.

For example, when we run a Java program, the JVM loads the bytecode, checks it, and executes it.


**********Real life Example :
JVM ko translator samjho.

Tum English mein bolte ho → saamne wala Marathi samajhta hai.

Translator tumhari English ko Marathi mein convert karta hai.

Similarly:

Bytecode → JVM → Machine instructions

